org: add a simplicity lens and machine-collected gate evidence

Two borrowings, both aimed at failure modes the current cycle cannot see.

From ponytail (already installed as a user skill, never wired into the org):
nothing in the pipeline asks whether the diff should have been written. Five
lenses can go green on code that should not exist. The ladder now runs at three
points — architect justifies each new file/abstraction/dependency, implementer
climbs it before writing, and a fifth `simplicity` review lens (plus
`/ponytail-review`) hunts what to cut. Simplicity findings never block the PR;
cheap ones get applied, the rest are filed as R rows.

From ECC: enforcement that lives outside the model's context. Two hooks —
`gate_evidence.py` (PostToolUse) appends every verification-bar command, exit
code and output tail to `.claude/evidence/<branch>.log` as it actually ran, so
the PR evidence section quotes a machine trail instead of an agent's account of
its own gates; `protected_paths.py` (PreToolUse) asks before an edit lands on a
CLAUDE.md protected path, moving that failure from a wasted cycle to a prompt.
CI's `guard` job stays the authority — the hook is a fast-fail, not a gate.

The Bash arm of the guard only fires when a write verb precedes the path, so
`pytest tests/integration/test_dryrun_safety.py | tee log` is not flagged.

