$ cd "/Users/bibekshrestha/Visual Studio/Admin_tookit" && git push -q -u origin org/ponytail-and-hook-evidence 2>&1 | tail -3 && gh pr create --base main --head org/ponytail-and-hook-evidence --title "org: simplicity lens (ponytail) + machine-collected gate evidence (hooks)" --body "$(cat <<'MD'
Two borrowings evaluated against the org as it stands, not adopted wholesale.

## What was taken

**From [ponytail](https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail)** — the skill was already installed globally but had never been wired into the cycle. Nothing in the pipeline currently asks *whether the diff should have been written*: all five gates and four review lenses can go green on code that should not exist. The ladder now runs at three points:

- `architect` — one line of justification for each new file, abstraction, dependency; if it can't be written, the plan has a rung too many.
- `implementer` — climb the ladder before writing; `ponytail:` comment for a deliberate shortcut with a known ceiling.
- `reviewer` — new fifth lens **simplicity**, reporting *what to cut → what replaces it*; plus `/ponytail-review` alongside `/code-review` and `/security-review` in phase 4.

Simplicity findings **never block the PR** (only correctness/security/regression do). Cheap ones are applied in the diff, the rest filed as `R` rows. The lens cuts what the diff added; it does not re-open a settled design.

**From [ECC](https://github.com/affaan-m/ecc)** — the one idea worth having is enforcement *outside* the model's context. ECC's 286 skills / 68 agents / 94 commands are a breadth play; this repo has six sharp agents and a constitution, and importing that catalogue would be pure context bloat. Its hooks layer is the exception:

- `.claude/hooks/gate_evidence.py` (PostToolUse) — appends every verification-bar command, exit code and output tail to `.claude/evidence/<branch>.log` (gitignored) as it actually ran. Phase 5 now says to quote that file in the PR evidence section: the board is already told not to trust an implementer's self-reported kill table, and self-reported green gates deserve the same treatment. A gate not in the log did not run.
- `.claude/hooks/protected_paths.py` (PreToolUse) — asks before an edit lands on a CLAUDE.md protected path. CI's `guard` job stays the authority; this only moves the failure from one wasted cycle to one prompt. It *asks* rather than denies, because a human in the session may edit these files and a headless subagent (which cannot answer) may not.

## Evidence

- `ruff check .claude/hooks/` + `ruff format --check` — clean.
- `ruff check ts_admin/ tests/` + `ruff format --check ts_admin/ tests/` — `All checks passed!`, `114 files already formatted`. No product code is touched, so the remaining gates are unaffected by this diff.
- Both hooks driven with synthetic PreToolUse/PostToolUse payloads:
  - `sed -i ... CLAUDE.md` → ask · `cat > ts_admin/main.py` → ask · `echo x >> .github/workflows/ci.yml` → ask
  - `cat CLAUDE.md` → silent · `git diff .github/workflows/ci.yml` → silent · `pytest tests/integration/test_dryrun_safety.py -v | tee /tmp/l` → **silent** (write verb must precede the path, or QA's own gate run would be blocked)
  - `Edit ts_admin/services/user_service.py` → silent
  - gate-evidence hook on a `ruff check` payload → wrote the expected `=== <ts> exit=0 / $ cmd / output` block.
- Found and fixed in the live session: the first settings.json wired the hook command unquoted, and this project's path contains a space (`Visual Studio/`), which broke every tool call until the command was quoted. Verified working after the fix.

## Not taken

ECC's rules packs, memory vault, and multi-harness adapters — `docs/org-memory/` + `CLAUDE.md` already cover that ground for one repo and one harness. A simplicity **hunting lens** for `discover` was also left out: `/ponytail-audit` can do a repo-wide pass on request, and B2/B3 exist to keep the queue from absorbing more rows.

Touches no protected path and no product code. `guard` should be green.

